  "item_id": "2c2f305856a15081f2fe9066",
  "version_id": "2c18cdc1f7cd0b9f4f773d6fa5733d29d115f5752e8ad9fe28d99ad61680389e",
  "synopsis": "This author-reported preprint contributes experimental methods and comparisons for pieces 01–03: delegating investigations through persistent records, evaluating research policies against final outcomes, and assessing training resources and reproducibility. Its evidentiary role is a primary experimental report, not independent validation. Distinguish frozen-weight MIRA orchestration from MIRA-AC adapter training. The selected evidence supports practical discussion of work orders, retained failures, baseline controls, evaluation boundaries and incomplete cost accounting. All subsequent substantive claims still require a validated passage citation.",
  "limitations_and_counterevidence": "Token-matched continuation recovers most of the reported theorem-proving advantage. Environment-specific proxy-trained MIRA-AC solves fewer BNLearn tasks than untrained MIRA, while the cross-environment configuration improves that count; these configurations must not be conflated. BNLearn enters critic pretraining, scientific policy evaluation reuses task identities and seeds, and BNLearn proxy and gold scoring share a hidden target. One policy is trained per configuration. Context-cost comparisons are incomplete replay projections, not measured total-cost savings. Behavioral judgments are descriptive and require human review as an editorial safeguard, not because bias has been demonstrated. The supplied report does not provide independent replication or external researcher assessment. These orientation notes do not substitute for passage-level evidence.",
  "passages": [
    {
      "purpose": "Delegation and inspectability — Appendix C.2 describes executable work orders, validation, commits, handovers and preservation of contradictory evidence.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "A fresh executor in the Codex harness receives the work order, its selected repository references, and the stable environment instructions. It may inspect and modify the workspace, run tests and authorized tools, and evaluate candidates. It validates substantive work, commits useful artifacts and evidence, and leaves a concise handover. Recorded failures and contradictory results remain available to later decisions through the repository.\nThe repository—including the current candidate, experiments, results, analyses, and handovers—persists across iterations; the model contexts do not. Any proxy evaluation produced after execution becomes evidence for the next decision. When an environment maintains an incumbent, it protects the strongest validated artifact against later regressions."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Actual training boundary — Appendix H identifies LoRA weight updates, fixed executors, initialization differences and BNLearn exposure through four-environment critic pretraining.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "All policy-training runs use Qwen3.6-27B ([Qwen Team, 2026](#bib.bib73))\n(Qwen-27B) with LoRA adapters. In separate actor–critic configurations, actor\nadapters target the standard attention and MLP projections,\nmixture-of-experts (MoE) experts, and latent fully connected projections. MIRA-AC targets the\nstandard attention and MLP projections together with the model’s recurrent\ninput and output projections. Separate-critic variants use distinct policy and\ncritic adapters. In MIRA-AC, one shared adapter serves both roles, with separate\noptimizer states for actor and critic updates; online critic updates modify only\nthe shared adapter’s MLP LoRA parameters. Each critic is initialized from a pretrained checkpoint\nmatched to its critic parameterization and target environment.\nSeparate actors start from the untrained MIRA policy. For each single-environment\nexperiment, MIRA-AC initializes its shared adapter from a generative critic\npretrained on trajectories from that environment under the matching supervision\ntarget. The cross-environment policy instead starts from a proxy-marginal\ngenerative critic pretrained jointly across all four environments, then receives\nonline actor–critic updates on SRBench, Physics Discovery, and\nHillClimbBench-CPU."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Reported performance and essential baseline qualification — §6.1 pairs the theorem-proving result with the continuation control that recovers most of the gap.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "On IMOProofBench Advanced, we count a problem as solved only when the grader\nawards full credit. Using GPT-5.5, MIRA raises the\nmean normalized grader score from 67.1% under direct inference\nto 100%, solving all 30 problems in\neach of three trials. With Qwen3.6-27B ([Qwen Team, 2026](#bib.bib73)),\nMIRA also more than doubles\nthe model’s score\n(Fig. [4](#S6.F4)(a)).\nAll conditions receive the same runtime budget, but MIRA makes better use of\nits inference budget to iterate on its solution, while the baselines submit\nmuch earlier. To determine whether its gains come from this allocation or\nsimply from generating more tokens, we force the baselines to continue until\nthey match MIRA’s output-token count on each problem. This recovers\nmost of the performance gap, but the residual gap\nis concentrated on four problems."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Comparative results and counterevidence — §6.3 preserves column labels and all configurations so gains cannot obscure the BNLearn deterioration or differences between success counts and continuous scores.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "|  |  | Symbolic Regression |  | Physics Discovery |  | HillClimbBench-CPU |  | BNLearn |  | \n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|  | Training variant | Solved | \\(R^{2}\\) | Solved | Law qual. | Wins | Arch. qual. | Solved | SHD gain | \n|  | MIRA (untrained) | 50 | 0.9864 | 8 | 0.2118 | — | 0.7624 | 76 | 0.9672 | \n| Gold binary | Token regression | 55 | 0.9957 | 9 | 0.2327 | 49 | 0.7594 | 79 | 0.9567 | \n|  | Step generative | 58 | 0.9993 | 9 | 0.2733 | 43 | 0.7518 | 82 | 0.9804 | \n|  | MIRA-AC | 65 | 0.9879 | 6 | 0.1846 | 49 | 0.7481 | 56 | 0.9261 | \n| Proxy marginal | Token regression | 55 | 0.9860 | 6 | 0.2463 | 49 | 0.7618 | 63 | 0.9002 | \n|  | Step regression | 50 | 0.9980 | 6 | 0.3043 | 46 | 0.7578 | 84 | 0.9733 | \n|  | Step generative | 59 | 0.9990 | 8 | 0.2463 | 53 | 0.7693 | 76 | 0.9106 | \n|  | MIRA-AC | 60 | 0.9994 | 10 | 0.2955 | 58 | 0.8054 | 48 | 0.9059 | \n|  | Online actor training across all environments except BNLearn |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  | \n|  | MIRA-AC | 63 | 0.9869 | 10 | 0.3028 | 55 | 0.7853 | 84 | 0.9869 | "
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Generalization limit — Appendix B.1 explicitly distinguishes new trajectories from unseen scientific task identities.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "For the three scientific environments, policy training and reported evaluation use independently initialized trajectories on the same fixed task identities and seeds. They therefore test new research trajectories and held-out evaluations within the same tasks, not generalization to unseen task identities."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Evaluation boundary — Appendix B.2 qualifies what hidden gold evaluation means, including BNLearn's shared hidden target.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "After\nselection, a hidden gold evaluation produces the score reported in the results\ntable. It uses the same hidden target as the proxy for BNLearn and separate\nheld-out data or conditions for the other environments. A task is solved when\nthis gold evaluation meets the environment-specific success criterion."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Concrete proxy failure — Appendix K.4 reports a reversal between validation improvement and gold performance, while retaining the authors' allocation-versus-execution qualification.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "The proxy gain was substantial: validation NMSE fell from \\(6.89\\times 10^{-4}\\) to \\(6.08\\times 10^{-7}\\) while satisfying the relative-error criterion. Retaining the model was therefore reasonable given the available metrics, despite the known extrapolation risk. The gold result reversed the proxy ordering: untrained MIRA scored 0.9998 and MIRA-AC scored 0. The executor fitted and compared the candidate equations, but the outer policy directly set the criterion for choosing the final equation and selected the formula after naming its weakness. Forced continuation preserved later opportunities to work, but supplied neither the candidate equation nor the selection criterion."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Resources and reproducibility limit — Appendix H reports the general configuration's hardware and one trained policy per configuration, with episode counts and seed procedure.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "| Compute | 16 H200s for actor–critic inference and optimization, plus 16 H200s serving frozen executors | \nWe train one policy per configuration. Each environment-specific run makes one pass over a fixed ordered set of 100 episodes. The cross-environment run uses 300 episodes: 100 from each of its three actor-training environments. The scientific environments use 100 tasks with one rollout each; HillClimbBench-CPU uses 50 tasks with two rollouts each. Within each environment, the task order is deterministically shuffled from run seed 42; environments are interleaved by equal-weight deficit round robin, and generation seeds are tied to logical decision identifiers."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Cost-accounting qualification — Appendix H defines replay projections and omitted components; this cannot establish measured total-cost reductions.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "The context-cost analysis in Fig. [11](#S6.F11) compares\nMIRA-AC with fixed-trajectory replays that preserve the recorded work orders,\nexecutor actions, and tool interactions. The MIRA-AC baseline counts policy\ninput together with exact or imputed executor input. The no-compaction replay\ncarries all prior cross-iteration history into each later model call, while the auto-compaction\nprojection caps that history at 200k tokens. The displayed range captures how\noften the carried history is reread. Its lower endpoint adds the history once\nduring each active stage—curation, work-order selection, and execution. Its\nupper endpoint follows a sequential tool loop in which the updated context is\nread after each recorded curation request and executor turn. Frozen-curator\nbase prompts and outputs, critic inputs, compaction-summary costs, and\nbehavioral changes are not modeled. The comparison therefore concerns\nrollout-generation cost, not optimization cost or sample efficiency."
      }
    },
    {
      "purpose": "Trustworthiness of behavioral interpretation — §7 limits LLM-judge evidence to descriptive associations and identifies the need for human or task-specific evaluation and causal interventions.",
      "locator": {
        "type": "text",
        "quote": "The behavioral analysis should likewise be interpreted as descriptive. Its conclusions rely on an LLM judge assigning process labels and comparing matched trajectories. These annotations provide evidence that training changes observable research behavior, but they do not establish that any individual behavior—such as testing competing hypotheses, abandoning stalled directions, or making larger pivots—causes the associated performance difference. More direct interventions on research strategy, together with human or task-specific behavioral evaluation, would be needed to identify which learned behaviors are causally useful and under which research regimes."
      }
    }
  ]
}
